// A full point-in-time health view of a single cluster, served on the read path
// by ClusterService.WatchClusterHealth for the cluster detail pages (Overview /
// Components / Workload / Details tabs). This is the read-path projection of the
// operator's ClusterSnapshot: scoped to what the detail pages render, not the
// full operator push.
//
// Two sources feed it. The snapshot the operator last pushed through
// ClusterStatusService (cluster_health_snapshots) comes first: every field it
// carries is served. The cluster row's stored status (clusters.json_status,
// written by the UpdateStatus heartbeat or by the push) fills in what the push
// lacks — observed_at (the last heartbeat), versions, config, never cpu or memory —
// and decides health; the push alters that verdict only as ClusterSummary.health
// describes (a HEALTHY refined to DEGRADED, or UNHEALTHY once the push is stale).
// A section the operator could not collect is absent from its push and absent
// here, never faked; the repeated sections have no presence on the wire, so one
// reported empty is likewise absent. accelerators, node_pools, api_success_rate,
// nodes.used and pods.used come from the push alone: Status has no field for
// them, so they are unset for a cluster without one. nodes.capacity and pods.capacity are the
// managed-cluster record's node and pod ceilings, served with or without a push.
